hifiberry-soloist-wrapper (0.1.3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Declare conflicts_with: ["librespot"] in the player descriptor. Only one
    Spotify Connect endpoint may advertise at a time; soloist.service has
    carried Conflicts=librespot.service from the start, but that only stops
    the two running simultaneously. Nothing stopped both being *enabled*, so
    both started at boot and whichever won the race stopped the other,
    giving a different Spotify player on different boots.
  * Requires hifiberry-configurator 2.17.1, which passes the new field
    through /api/v1/players.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 09:30:00 +0000

hifiberry-soloist-wrapper (0.1.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Do not restart config-server from the postinst. When the WebUI installs
    an extension, config-server is the process running apt and it holds the
    job the UI polls in memory; restarting from the postinst -- which runs
    during dpkg's configure step, part-way through the install -- destroyed
    that job. apt survived (the runner starts it under systemd-run) so the
    package installed correctly, but the UI polled an id that no longer
    existed and sat on "Configuring" indefinitely.
    The restart was also unnecessary: configurator already reloads conf.d in
    place after every extension install, via refresh_system_state().

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000

hifiberry-soloist-wrapper (0.1.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Declare the extension metadata the WebUI catalog reads. 0.1.0 shipped
    only XB-Hifiberry-Extension, so the card fell back to every default:
    it showed the raw package name "hifiberry-soloist-wrapper" and was
    filed under "Tools" rather than "Players".
    - XB-Extension-Name: Spotify Soloist
    - XB-Extension-Category: player
    - XB-Extension-Needs-Reboot: no
    - XB-Extension-Icon-Url pointing at the spotify.svg that
      hifiberry-webui already ships, so it resolves without internet
  * Correct the SIGTERM handler's docstring, which described a
    PartOf=soloist.service that the bridge unit deliberately does not have.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 07:00:00 +0000

hifiberry-soloist-wrapper (0.1.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Initial release: registers Spotify Soloist as an external Spotify
    Connect player. Spotify's Soloist binary may not be redistributed, so
    this package never ships it -- it is downloaded from Spotify's CDN onto
    the device itself, on the user's request, using their own API key.
    - soloist-fetch downloads and installs the Soloist binary, verifies it
      runs, and records its version/build date for expiry tracking
    - start-soloist reads the user's API key from ConfigDB and launches
      Soloist as a Spotify Connect endpoint (systemd --user soloist.service,
      WebUI-toggleable, disabled by default; conflicts with librespot)
    - soloist-update, plus soloist-update.timer (daily) and
      soloist-update.path (expiry-flag-triggered), refresh the build before
      Spotify's 90-day expiry
    - soloist-bridge bridges Soloist's local WebSocket to audiocontrol for
      metadata, artwork, queue and transport control, and serves the
      WebUI's setup API (GET /status, POST /install) on 127.0.0.1:3549
    - ACR player descriptor and WebUI player card (Players page)
    - nginx drop-in proxying /api/soloist/status and /api/soloist/install,
      and the matching auth-gateway tier manifest
    - config-server permission drop-in for the soloist/soloist-bridge
      systemd units

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Wed, 19 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
